Adjudicating between genuine generalization and memorization is in the first instance a problem of experimental design rather than of interpretation, and the field's principal instrument has been the construction of a fresh test set that matches an older one in form but not in content. GSM8K comprises roughly 125,000 math word problems, and its publication preceded the construction of GSM1k. {{S1}}

What matters evidentially is how scores move when the items change. Either every model evaluated on GSM1k showed a statistically significant accuracy drop relative to GSM8K, or at least one model evaluated on GSM1k showed no statistically significant accuracy drop relative to GSM8K; exactly one of these two outcomes obtains, and they cannot both. At least one of two conclusions about a given high scorer then holds, and perhaps both of them do. A drop across every model confirms that the model's elevated benchmark score was driven in part by exposure to the benchmark items during pretraining; the preservation of accuracy in at least one model indicates that the elevated score was driven in part by reasoning ability acquired from data other than the benchmark.

Two further lines of work attack the problem before any re-testing takes place: the auditing of corpora for textual overlap, and the design of benchmarks whose contents could not have been seen. {{S2}}

{{S3}} A model that assigns materially higher likelihood to the canonical ordering has, on this reasoning, encountered the dataset file itself during training. GSM-Symbolic, which was developed and released by Google DeepMind, pursues a third route, regenerating each problem instance from a symbolic template, whereas LiveBench's protection rests on the recency of its source material.

Whereas the test of Oren and colleagues interrogates an already trained model after the fact, the remaining safeguards operate upstream, at the level of the corpus itself. {{S4}} The practical upshot is that the dispute is rarely settled by a single experiment: convergent results from held-out replications, likelihood-based probes, and procedural markers in the data are what move the field toward a verdict.

