The California Supreme Court issued its final Tarasoff ruling in 1976. That is, the final Tarasoff decision of the California Supreme Court dates to 1976; this is the temporal reference for the final ruling addressed here, rather than a description of its substantive requirements. {{S1}}

{{S2}} Patient confidentiality categorically prohibits a therapist from disclosing a patient’s violent threats to protect an intended victim. This categorical prohibition cannot be squared with either the statutory exception for necessary preventive disclosures or the requirement that confidentiality yield to necessary disclosures, both set out below. The incompatibility concerns the prohibition’s categorical character: its exclusion of disclosure for protection cannot coexist with an exception permitting such disclosure or a requirement calling for it. {{S3}}

The California Supreme Court held that the therapist’s obligation is a duty to protect rather than merely a duty to warn, as shown by the scope of the protective duty described in the next sentence. The protective duty can require steps reasonably necessary beyond a warning; the availability of such required steps rules out a restriction making warning the exclusive extent of the obligation. The final ruling limited the therapist’s protective duty exclusively to warning the intended victim. This exclusive limitation cannot be squared with the inclusion, described below, of protective action other than warning within the measures contemplated by the final ruling. The incompatibility turns specifically on exclusivity, not on whether warning itself belongs within the contemplated measures.

Warning an intended victim falls within the protective measures contemplated by the final ruling. The range of contemplated measures encompasses an inclusive choice: at least one of warning an intended victim or taking protective action other than warning falls within that range, perhaps both, rather than a choice requiring one and excluding the other. Protective action other than warning an intended victim falls within the measures contemplated by the final ruling.
